Trithemius Corpus — rights and licensing

This project combines original texts, translations, editorial work and
institutional images. Their terms are set out below.

----

1. The Latin source texts — public domain

   The historical Latin works by Trithemius and associated authors are in
   the public domain. The catalogue records in manifest.json identify the
   digitized copies and their institutional sources.

2. The English translations and the per-work generated artifacts —
   no copyright claimed (CC0 1.0 / public domain)

   Every translation in this corpus was produced by machine (large language
   models), as were the per-chunk grades, the researched headnotes, and the
   Style C cipher renderings. Material generated by an AI without human
   creative authorship is not protected by copyright (current U.S. Copyright
   Office guidance; Thaler v. Perlmutter, 2023). No copyright is therefore
   claimed in these outputs. To the extent any rights could attach, they are
   dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0:
   https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
   You may use them for any purpose, without permission or attribution
   (a citation is appreciated but not required — see below).

3. The methodology, documentation, and code — © 2026 Ian Carlos Fabin, CC BY 4.0

   The human-authored layer — the methodology essay, the README,
   the project documentation, the build scripts and
   site templates, and the original selection and arrangement of the corpus —
   is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License
   (CC BY 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
   You are free to share and adapt this material, including commercially,
   provided you give appropriate credit, link the license, and indicate
   whether changes were made.

4. Institutional images, crops and credited third-party files

   A scan, thumbnail or crop carries the holding institution's rights
   statement. When redistributing images, include their institutional
   credits, source URLs, rights URLs and any crop or adaptation details.
   The correction folders record these in facsimiles/images.json and
   facsimiles/crops.json.

   Stadtbibliothek Trier, T 288 8’, supplied through Dilibri: the Sigilla
   digitization (PRDL 70291), its WebP pages and thumbnails, the archived
   title page and its rectangular detail carry CC BY-NC-SA 3.0.
   Source: https://www.dilibri.de/ubtrdfg/content/titleinfo/466737
   Terms: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/

   Bayerische Staatsbibliothek: the Mainz comparison leaf used for the
   prayer in PRDL 24373, bsb11303506, canvas 9, carries No Copyright –
   Non-Commercial Use Only. The same statement applies to its detail.
   Source: https://api.digitale-sammlungen.de/iiif/presentation/v2/bsb11303506/canvas/9
   Terms: https://rightsstatements.org/vocab/NoC-NC/1.0/
   The Leipzig leaf displayed beside it, bsb00040424, canvas 4, carries
   the Public Domain Mark: https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/

   The unmodified Citation File Format 1.2.0 schema in data/schemas is by
   the Citation File Format contributors, CC BY 4.0.
   Source: https://github.com/citation-file-format/citation-file-format/blob/396f738fb025b1d8acdb02a56ffc923f95dc8999/schema.json
   Terms: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
   The bundled fonts carry the terms in site/static/fonts/LICENSE.txt.

----

How to cite (a credit, not a copyright claim):

  Fabin, Ian Carlos (2026). Trithemius Corpus: machine-assisted English translations
  of Latin texts by or associated with Johannes Trithemius.
  https://github.com/Trithemius-Corpus/Trithemius-Corpus

See `CITATION.cff` for a machine-readable citation record.
